    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay examines the recent history of government-supported tourism initiatives in Harlem, New York, and reveals how complex the balance between economic growth and cultural vitality can be. The author conducts interviews with local owners of tourism business to show that a focus on economic improvement in general can overlook the fact that local businesses get displaced and, worse, the local cultures can become harmfully spectacularized or selectively ignored.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
